%description:

RFC 1122 -- the rate of requests for one unresolved address is limited.

Checks RFC1122-AFLOOD-1 (must) from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc1122/catalog.md. The
English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/flood-prevention.md#the-request-rate; the program
step below maps onto its expected observations. Mapping of the absent neighbour mockup:
host A = hostA, host B = hostB, and 10.0.0.99 is the address that no station owns.

Host A sends a datagram every 0.1 s for 10 s to an address on its own link that nobody has.
Nothing ever answers, so the question can be asked again and again. A host with no mechanism
at all would put about 100 requests on the link. The recommended maximum of one per second
per destination allows 10 over the same window, and the check allows 11, so that a host may
send its first request at the moment the first datagram arrives.

Expected observations 1 and 3 are one greedy step: at least one request, and at most 11, in
the whole run.

Deviation: expected observation 2, that no reply for the address ever crosses the link, is
not a program step, because a greedy step consumes its whole window. It is asserted instead
on the line that the program of host B prints at the end of the run: host B received nothing,
so no datagram of host A reached any station, so no resolution succeeded.

Note on the bound: the MUST of RFC 1122 is about the mechanism, and the rate of one per
second is a recommendation. A host that chose a higher rate would fail this step and could
still satisfy the requirement. The verdict would then belong to the recommendation.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc1122_arp_flood_prevention")
        // Expected observations 1 and 3: between 1 and 11 requests for 10.0.0.99 in 10 s.
        .betweenTimes(1, 11, on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("arp.opcode == 1")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return ipv4AddressField(c.event.packet, "arp.destIpAddress") == "10.0.0.99";
                  })
                  .describe("an ARP request from host A for 10.0.0.99")
                  .within(9.9));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc1122_arp_flood_prevention compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The absent neighbour mockup: two hosts on one Ethernet link, and a third address on the
// link that no station owns.
network ArpPairNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> hostB.ethg++; // link 1
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpPairNet
sim-time-limit = 10s
seed-set = 0

# Fixed addresses, so that the address nobody owns is a known one.
*.configurator.config = xml("<config><interface hosts='hostA' address='10.0.0.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'/><interface hosts='hostB' address='10.0.0.2' netmask='255.255.255.0'/></config>")

# A datagram every 0.1 s to 10.0.0.99, which no station owns.
*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.hostA.app[0].destAddresses = "10.0.0.99"
*.hostA.app[0].destPort = 5000
*.hostA.app[0].localPort = 4000
*.hostA.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.hostA.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.hostA.app[0].sendInterval = 0.1s
*.hostB.numApps = 1
*.hostB.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
*.hostB.app[0].localPort = 5000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc1122_arp_flood_prevention: PASS

%contains: stdout
hostB.app[0]: received 0 packets
